ip  and we have this evidence of women who  have performed  very well and have managed  the pandemic in in an impressive way  you talked about women in the workplace  and the impact it's had and  of course in the uk it's also had you  know a big  big impact on how women are doing in the  workplace the pandemic so for example  you know 17 mothers in the uk so they've  had to  give up on work during the pandemic uh  mothers on the lowest incomes nine times  more likely  to have lost their jobs and do you worry  that  even in places like the uk and wealthy  nations that the pandemic could  set back women's progress potentially  decades  oh absolutely i mean just for women  sleeping  out of the labor market is  is a big issue and in countries like the  uk where women  did achieve much better representation  in the labor  market it is going to cost us  a a lot to have those  those women now not spending and not  being able to be as independent as they  were  as a as working mothers working women  another aspect that we've seen in the uk  is in domestic violence  the number of people reporting uh  domestic violence the un's described it  as a shadow pandemic how concerned are  you that as we're told to stay at home  it's actually not a safe place for so  many women we have  called this interest  of gender-based violence a pandemic  of its own a shadow pandemic that has  existed  before there was a covert 19  and will continue to exist after private  19 so we need to fight it not just for  what is happening now during the pending  but fight it  for the future as well and deal with it  as a pandemic with all hands on deck in  the context of gender-based violence  our frontline workers are social workers  the police  civil society the judiciary  all of those we need to make decisions  that will prevent  the violence from happening as well as  prosecute  in some countries the increase has been  as high  as 40 percent i'm also interested as  well if you're worried about  uh increased nationalism the country is  effectively looking to  protect themselves because they're  dealing with the crisis  of covid rather than reaching out and  trying to help uh people  in other countries so in the uk for  example you know we've reduced the aid  budget from 0.7 to 0.5  of gdp we've cut aid to yemen  how much of a concern is this for the  u.n it really is concerning us and we  are appealing  for countries to be generous at this  time of need  a solidarity in a time like this  is actually important because in in the  fight against the pandemic  a safety in one country  when there is no safety in another  country is not real safety  we need to fight the pandemic all over  the world and to help each other  because we will not be able to be safer  if one part of the world  is not safe the same thing about driving  countries into  extreme poverty we know that when people  are desperate  they will move around the world so the  people that you are  living exposed to extreme poverty will  show up  on your desktop at some point so we have  to be  a in this all together we are also  concerned about women who are refugees  and migrants and in people who are  refugees and migrants  because covert has tended to push people  out of countries that are hosting them  back to their own countries where  sometimes they do not have the  infrastructure  to survive in their own countries  because they already migrated  to a new countries and sometimes they  don't have the papers to be moving  back and forth so migrants are finding  themselves in very difficult situations  we're also concerned about  equitable distribution of vaccine  because there is  a danger also that  women in some situations  will not be given first preference for  accessing the vaccine if we know about  the preference for for boys the  preference  for men men could easily be in front of  of the line claiming that they are the  ones who are  maybe going out to work confining women  in the home space  which comes with its own complications  so we want governments to pay attention  to executable  distribution of the vaccine and make  sure that women  are not discriminated against just  finally  when i was researching you at the  interview i was just very struck by your  own sort of background  um growing up in south africa a teacher  becoming south africa's first female  deputy president at president  also having five children it's a pretty  impressive cv  and what is it about your own experience  uh growing up  and becoming a a woman that really sort  of drove you  and has inspired you you know i grew up  in  in south africa it's a time of  apartheid so you were thrust into  activism  because of the situation of the country  i also had a mother who was an activist  in her own right  so there was a push from the home front  but also  as a student by friends and  fellow students where activists in the  main and in fact  for people of my age group if you are  not  an activist you really  were somehow on the wrong track  because everything pointed you in a  direction  uh that called on you to take action  about what was happening  in the country it was much easier to be  an activist than to fold your heads  that's very interesting and thank you so  much for being on the program today and  talk a bit about international women's  day we appreciate it  thank you
 

